Feature visualization
=====================

PyTorch examples for https://henrikaalto.fi/blog/feature-visualization.
Run each chapter's Python module from this repository's root with uv.
The examples use a frozen Inception v1 to score the generated images.

Main Timeline
-------------

2009: First Images
    uv run --locked python -m recipes.first_images_2009

2013: Weight Decay
    uv run --locked python -m recipes.weight_decay_2013

2014: Total Variation
    uv run --locked python -m recipes.total_variation_2014

2015: Gaussian Blur
    uv run --locked python -m recipes.gaussian_blur_2015

2015: Random Shifts
    uv run --locked python -m recipes.random_shifts_2015

2017: Fourier
    uv run --locked python -m recipes.fourier_2017

2023: MACO
    uv run --locked python -m recipes.maco_2023

    Uses the MACO decoder and fixed ImageNet magnitudes with the 2017
    transforms and Adam schedule, as in the article's figure.

2026: Path Guidance
    The Gaussian-centered Fourier example shown in this section runs with:

    uv run --locked python -m recipes.centered_fourier_2026

    Path guidance itself is omitted. It uses reference images and
    activation routes derived from them.

Other techniques
----------------

2016: DGN-AM
    uv run --locked python -m weights.dgn
    uv run --locked python -m recipes.dgn_am_2016

    Setup downloads the original DeePSiM generator archive (~1.2 GiB)
    and extracts the fc6 weights. It also fetches the published latent
    bounds. The weights are for research use under the upstream terms.

2018: CPPNs
    uv run --locked python -m recipes.cppns_2018

2022: Classifier as Generator
    uv run --locked python -m weights.mae
    uv run --locked --with timm==1.0.22 python -m recipes.classifier_as_generator_2022

    Setup downloads the official MAE source and visualization weights
    (~427 MiB). This example uses the blog's frozen MAE adaptation with
    a 224-pixel reconstruction and a center-neuron objective. Here,
    --steps controls the number of updates per resolution stage.

2024: DiG-IN
    uv run --locked --with diffusers==0.35.2 --with transformers==4.56.2 --with accelerate==1.10.1 python -m recipes.dig_in_2024

    The first run fetches Stable Diffusion v1.4 weights. A GPU is
    recommended. Pass --model for a local Diffusers checkpoint and
    --prompt to choose the text conditioning. The three targets from the
    blog have preset prompts, so no dataset ranking or captioner is needed.

Different Objectives
--------------------

The centered Fourier example also exposes neuron, channel, and layer
objectives. For the layer objective, the channel index is ignored.

    uv run --locked python -m recipes.centered_fourier_2026 --objective neuron
    uv run --locked python -m recipes.centered_fourier_2026 --objective channel
    uv run --locked python -m recipes.centered_fourier_2026 --objective layer --target mixed3a:0 --strength .15

Each command supports --help, --target LAYER:CHANNEL, --device, --steps,
and --output DIRECTORY. By default, the images go under output/ with
one directory per technique. Reduce --steps to try a quick run.

Assets
------

The Inception weights were extracted from the frozen Lucid graph:
https://storage.googleapis.com/modelzoo/vision/other_models/InceptionV1.pb

The fixed magnitude prior is from Xplique. Its source, checksum, and
license are in assets/maco_imagenet.json.

See LICENSES/ for the upstream notices. Additional model downloads are
cached outside the tracked files.
